<section class="reference-section" id="devi-shumbha-nishumbha"><div class="reference-section-heading"><h2>Devi and Shumbha–Nishumbha</h2><a href="/devi-shumbha-nishumbha.html">Return to story</a></div><div class="reference-content">
<p><strong>Markandeya Purana · Devi Mahatmya · Chapters 5–11.</strong> This original condensed retelling was checked against both owner-supplied editions listed below. The corresponding Markandeya Purana cantos in Pargiter’s numbering are 85–91. The private source files are not copied into the public website or repository.</p>
<ol>
<li><strong>Concise Markandeya Purana · Gita Press, Gorakhpur · code 539 · Hindi edition.</strong><small>Devi Mahatmya sequence in the supplied scan, printed pages 217–245. Contents entries 108–125 identify the Goddess’s manifestation, the message to Shumbha, Dhumralochana, Chanda and Munda, Chamunda, the divine energies, Raktabija, Nishumbha, the withdrawal of the powers, Shumbha’s defeat, the gods’ hymn and the boon.</small></li>
<li><strong>The Markandeya Purana · F. E. Pargiter, translator · 1904 · English.</strong><small>Cantos 85–91, printed pages 488–518 in the supplied scan. Used to cross-check narrative order, names and the English prose summary.</small></li>
</ol>
<details><summary>Passages behind the key moments<span aria-hidden="true">+</span></summary><ul><li><strong>Kaushiki and the message:</strong> Devi Mahatmya chapter 5 / Markandeya canto 85.</li><li><strong>Dhumralochana:</strong> chapter 6 / canto 86.</li><li><strong>Chanda, Munda and the name Chamunda:</strong> chapter 7 / canto 87.</li><li><strong>The divine energies and Raktabija:</strong> chapter 8 / canto 88.</li><li><strong>Nishumbha:</strong> chapter 9 / canto 89.</li><li><strong>The withdrawal of the manifestations and Shumbha’s defeat:</strong> chapter 10 / canto 90.</li><li><strong>The Narayani hymn and requested protection:</strong> chapter 11 / canto 91.</li></ul></details>
<p>Names use readable English spellings. The text’s Kaushiki, Ambika, Chandika, Kali, Chamunda and Shivaduti designations are retained as forms or names within this episode; the page does not flatten them into a single visual inventory. Battle catalogues and graphic details are condensed. The reflection on changing strategy is editorial commentary, not a translated verse.</p>
<p>The four illustrations are newly generated devotional interpretations. The hero suggests the many energies without claiming a literal portrait of every Matrika; the Chamunda and Raktabija scenes avoid graphic violence; and the final duel is an imagined non-historical tableau.</p>
</div></section>
